WEBVTT

NOTE Cue timings are estimated from sentence length and pauses in the audio.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.687
Welcome to the Concilium Security Risk Brief for Monday, October nineteenth.

2
00:00:04.687 --> 00:00:12.277
Five minutes of security coverage from the last twenty four hours, to increase your situational awareness without decreasing your productivity.

3
00:00:12.277 --> 00:00:15.639
Here's what matters today.

4
00:00:15.639 --> 00:00:18.991
We start with Saudi Arabia, where governments are now pointing travelers to land routes out.

5
00:00:18.991 --> 00:00:28.522
Canada says that because of disruptions at several Saudi airports, anyone who wants to leave by air should consider travelling by land to an airport in a neighbouring country.

6
00:00:28.522 --> 00:00:36.006
Australia continues to advise against all travel to Saudi Arabia and says those who wish to leave should consider land transport.

7
00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:50.261
The State Department travel account is telling Americans across the Middle East to exercise heightened vigilance and to expect possible flight cancellations, airspace closures and travel disruptions, citing Houthi hostilities against Saudi Arabia.

8
00:00:50.261 --> 00:01:03.851
Staying with the conflict, the Critical Threats Project and the Institute for the Study of War assess the Houthis shot down a Saudi light attack helicopter over Saudi airspace that was likely supporting fighting in nearby al Jawf, Yemen.

9
00:01:03.851 --> 00:01:06.556
The Saudi-led coalition gave a different account.

10
00:01:06.556 --> 00:01:16.390
Dawn, citing the Saudi Press Agency, reports the coalition said a National Guard reconnaissance helicopter crashed in Najran because of a technical malfunction.

11
00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:22.747
Egyptian President Sisi announced that Egypt will provide defensive military aid to Saudi Arabia if asked.

12
00:01:22.747 --> 00:01:33.490
And the Critical Threats Project reports the United Arab Emirates agreed to resume salary payments for some Yemeni government-aligned forces, according to Yemeni sources cited by UK media.

13
00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:44.567
In Russia, the Institute for the Study of War says Ukrainian forces carried out their largest strike on digital, energy and logistics infrastructure in Saint Petersburg since twenty twenty two.

14
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:49.541
Radio Free Europe video shows drones hitting an oil depot and a warehouse in the city.

15
00:01:49.541 --> 00:01:58.490
The Institute says Russia, for its part, launched three hundred thirty seven drones and one Iskander ballistic missile against Ukraine over the same period.

16
00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:04.509
In Pakistan, the newspaper Dawn reports an army major, thirty four year old Wajahat Waheed, was killed in a targeted improvised explosive device attack in Balochistan.

17
00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:13.750
Global Decipher reports it happened during a clearance operation in Gwadar District.

18
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:21.235
In Sudan, the International Committee of the Red Cross says a Sudanese Red Crescent volunteer, Badr al-Din Omar Musa, was killed while carrying out humanitarian duties in the Blue Nile Region.

19
00:02:21.235 --> 00:02:27.582
The Red Cross called the continued loss of humanitarian personnel unacceptable.

20
00:02:27.582 --> 00:02:29.849
A few updates on stories we've covered.

21
00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:39.932
Update on Yemen: the Critical Threats Project says Houthi forces kept trying to advance on the western and southern outskirts of Taiz City.

22
00:02:39.932 --> 00:02:42.090
Now a few quick notes by region.

23
00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:50.567
In the Middle East and North Africa, Radio Free Europe reports Iran's parliament approved Mehrdad Akhlaqi Ketabchi as the new defense minister, by two hundred thirty seven votes to fifteen.

24
00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:57.493
Dawn reports he warned of enormous damage if the United States attacks.

25
00:02:57.493 --> 00:03:08.302
In Europe, the Institute for the Study of War assesses Russia continues to expand kinetic actions in Europe that are likely meant to disrupt Western help to Ukraine and test NATO's resolve.

26
00:03:08.302 --> 00:03:09.741
That's today's brief.

27
00:03:09.741 --> 00:03:17.797
These security developments come from government travel advisories and trusted analysts, curated by the Concilium Security Risk Management Team.

28
00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:25.868
The brief summarizes public reporting and isn't a substitute for your own risk assessment, so check the original source before making travel decisions.

29
00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:27.983
This brief is digitally narrated.

30
00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:28.946
Questions?

31
00:03:28.946 --> 00:03:32.430
Reach Concilium at info at concilium dot U.S.

32
00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.494
Thanks for listening, and stay safe.
